  <h2>Fabric in one click</h2>
  <p>Open GDLauncher, click <strong>+ New Instance</strong>, pick a Minecraft version, and select <strong>Fabric</strong> as the loader. GDLauncher downloads and installs the right Fabric version, the matching Java runtime, and you're ready to mod. <a href="/guides/install-fabric">Step-by-step Fabric guide →</a></p>

  <h2>Why Fabric is the modern choice for performance</h2>
  <p>Fabric is built around being lightweight, and most of the modern performance scene grew up around it. <strong>Sodium</strong> (rewritten chunk renderer for substantially higher FPS), <strong>Lithium</strong> (game logic optimization), and <strong>Iris</strong> (a Sodium-compatible shader loader) all shipped Fabric-first. <strong>FerriteCore</strong> (memory reduction) is multi-loader but a staple of the Fabric stack. Combined, they noticeably improve frame rates and load times compared to vanilla.</p>
  <p>Fabric also updates within hours of a new Minecraft release. Forge can take weeks. If you want to play the latest version, Fabric is usually ready first.</p>

  <h2>Top Fabric mods to install</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fabric API</strong>: Required dependency for most Fabric mods. Auto-installed by GDLauncher when needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Sodium</strong>: Rewritten chunk renderer for substantially higher FPS.</li>
    <li><strong>Lithium</strong>: Server-side game logic optimization.</li>
    <li><strong>Iris</strong>: Sodium-compatible shader loader. Use it instead of OptiFine.</li>
    <li><strong>Mod Menu</strong>: In-game mod settings UI.</li>
    <li><strong>FerriteCore</strong>: Reduces RAM use without changing gameplay.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fabric vs Forge in GDLauncher</h2>
  <p>You can have both. GDLauncher's instances are isolated, one Fabric instance for performance and Iris shaders, another Forge or NeoForge instance for big modpacks. Switch between them with one click. <a href="/forge-launcher">See the Forge launcher overview →</a></p>

